"""Sequenced, original beginner teaching for chapters 9-16.

Examples deliberately use different domains and function contracts from the quests.
Each Python example is self-contained; local terminal/file illustrations are labeled.
"""


def lesson(title, text, code=None, output=None, walk=None, check=None, shell=False):
    item = dict(title=title, text=text)
    if code is not None:
        item["code"] = code
    if output is not None:
        item["output"] = output
    if walk:
        item["walk"] = walk
    if check:
        item["check"] = check
    if shell:
        item["shell"] = True
    return item


CHAPTERS = {
    9: [
        lesson("Before choosing a pattern: describe the remaining work",
               "An algorithm is a sequence of steps for solving a problem. A pattern is a useful shape of algorithm, not a function to memorize. This chapter introduces positions moving through data, a stack of unfinished work, a moving range, and a shrinking search interval. Learn them separately before choosing one.\n\nAn invariant is a statement that remains true as a loop runs. Write one sentence describing what your variables mean before the loop, after one iteration, and at the end. This gives you a reason for each update and a way to find a bug. You already know lists, indexes, while, and comparisons; those are enough to build these patterns.",
               check="For a list of four items, name its first and last valid indexes. What index represents the last item of an empty list? Why must you avoid reading it?"),
        lesson("Two positions and a simultaneous swap",
               "A pointer here is just an integer index; it is not a special Python type. One index can start at the left edge and another at the right. left < right means there is still a distinct pair to inspect. After handling that pair, left += 1 and right -= 1 move inward.\n\nIn a multiple assignment, Python evaluates the right-hand values before assigning either left-hand destination. That lets a, b = b, a swap values safely. list(text) creates a list of characters because strings themselves cannot be changed. The string method separator.join(parts) joins strings using that separator; an empty separator joins without spaces.",
               'letters = list("lamp")\nleft = 0\nright = len(letters) - 1\nletters[left], letters[right] = letters[right], letters[left]\nprint(letters)\nleft += 1\nright -= 1\nprint(left, right)\nprint("".join(letters))',
               "['p', 'a', 'm', 'l']\n1 2\npaml",
               ["The right-hand side first reads the old last and first characters: p and l.", "The assignments change only those two positions. The middle pair has not been handled yet.", "This is one step of a reversal, not a completed algorithm. Draw the next pair yourself."],
               "What statement describes the positions outside left and right after a completed step?"),
        lesson("A stack: append puts on, pop takes off",
               "A stack removes the most recently added item first: last in, first out. Python lists already provide the operations. stack.append(value) adds one value at the end; stack.pop() removes the last value AND returns it. Unlike append, pop has a useful returned value.\n\nCalling pop on an empty list raises IndexError. Check whether a list is nonempty before popping. In a condition, [] is false and a nonempty list is true. The last item can also be inspected with stack[-1] without removing it, but that too needs a nonempty stack. An undo history is a natural stack: the newest unfinished action is the first one undone.",
               'history = []\nhistory.append("draw circle")\nhistory.append("draw square")\nprint(history[-1])\nif history:\n    undone = history.pop()\n    print("undo:", undone)\nprint(history)',
               "draw square\nundo: draw square\n['draw circle']",
               check="After two pushes and one pop, how many unfinished actions remain? What should code do before attempting another pop?"),
        lesson("Nesting requires order, not just equal counts",
               "Nested structures must close in reverse opening order. A cupboard inside a room must be left before the room is left. A stack remembers this unfinished work. Merely counting openings and closings misses a closing symbol that appears before its opening.\n\nSeparate three situations when designing a nested checker: a new opening, a closing when something is waiting, and a closing when nothing is waiting. At the end, decide what leftover openings mean. If the task supports several symbol types, remember which opening type is waiting, not only how many. If the contract says to ignore other text, ordinary letters should leave the state unchanged. This explains the model; write your exercise's own decisions and tests yourself.",
               check="Without coding, compare () and )( . They have equal counts. Why do they need different results?"),
        lesson("A window is a consecutive slice",
               "A window is a consecutive part of a sequence. A fixed-size window keeps exactly k items. In values[start:stop], start is included and stop is excluded, so the length is stop - start when those indexes are valid. sum(values[0:3]) adds the first three values.\n\nAs a window moves one position right, exactly one item leaves and one arrives. You can update the previous total instead of summing the whole slice again. Keep the meaning of the endpoints explicit. First validate the requested size: zero, negative, or too large cannot describe a real window under this chapter's contract.",
               'readings = [4, -2, 7, 1]\nfirst = readings[0:2]\nwindow_total = sum(first)\nprint(first, window_total)\noutgoing = readings[0]\nincoming = readings[2]\nwindow_total = window_total - outgoing + incoming\nprint(readings[1:3], window_total)',
               "[4, -2] 2\n[-2, 7] 5",
               ["The first window contains indexes 0 and 1, totaling 2.", "Moving right removes 4 and adds 7; -2 remains inside.", "The new total is 2 - 4 + 7 = 5. The slice confirms which values it describes."],
               "If all window totals are negative, why is zero a misleading starting value for a best-so-far total?"),
        lesson("Best-so-far values need a real starting candidate",
               "A running maximum remembers the greatest candidate seen so far. Starting at zero silently assumes that zero is a valid candidate or that some candidate cannot be smaller. That assumption fails for all-negative data. Once you have confirmed a nonempty set of candidates, initialize from its first actual candidate.\n\nAn empty result needs its own policy before this initialization. In the window exercise, None means the requested size is invalid; it is not the same as a total of zero. Keep 'no result exists' separate from 'the result exists and equals zero'.",
               'candidates = [-8, -3, -6]\nbest = candidates[0]\nfor candidate in candidates[1:]:\n    if candidate > best:\n        best = candidate\nprint(best)',
               "-3",
               check="Trace best after every candidate. Which wrong result would initializing best = 0 produce?"),
        lesson("Binary search: ordered data lets you discard a half",
               "Binary search needs data already sorted according to the comparison you use. In an inclusive interval, low and high are both candidate indexes. The middle index is (low + high) // 2; // keeps a whole-number index. Compare the middle value with the target, not the middle index with the target.\n\nIf the middle value is too small, sorted order proves everything at or left of it is too small. The next low is mid + 1. If it is too large, the next high is mid - 1. Excluding the already-checked middle guarantees progress. When low > high, no candidate remains. Do not sort the input silently: the task's indexes refer to its original supplied order.",
               'shelf = [3, 6, 10, 14, 19, 25, 31]\nwanted = 25\nlow = 0\nhigh = len(shelf) - 1\nmid = (low + high) // 2\nprint(mid, shelf[mid])\nlow = mid + 1  # 14 was too small\nmid = (low + high) // 2\nprint(mid, shelf[mid])',
               "3 14\n5 25",
               ["Initially candidate indexes are 0 through 6, inclusive.", "Index 3 holds 14, so the second probe uses only indexes 4 through 6.", "Index 5 holds 25. These are two probes, not a completed search function."],
               "Draw the intervals for a missing target. How does low > high represent an empty range?"),
        lesson("Practice and lab: test the invariant, not a memorized example",
               "Use separate trace tables for each pattern. A two-pointer table needs left, right, compared items, and the next indexes. A stack table needs the current item and the unfinished stack. A window table needs its range and total. A binary-search table needs low, high, mid, and the discarded region.\n\nTry empty and size-one inputs where allowed, missing targets, reversed nesting, and all-negative windows. The browser exercises have precise contracts; for example, text comparisons may deliberately be case-sensitive. Do not add cleaning rules that were never requested. For the independent lab, write each function's invariant in your README and change one requirement only after the original tests pass.",
               check="Before coding, identify which practice tasks require sorted data and which must preserve the supplied order."),
    ],
    10: [
        lesson("An object is state plus operations",
               "A function is often enough for a calculation. A class is useful when individual things keep state across several operations. A class defines a type; calling the class creates an instance of that type. An attribute is a named value belonging to an object. A method is a function accessed through an object. The dot in lamp.brightness reads the brightness attribute of lamp, just as a dictionary lookup reads a value by a key.\n\nStart with one object and one attribute. You do not need inheritance or a large architecture to use a class. The goal is to make ownership of changing values clear.",
               'class Lamp:\n    def __init__(self, brightness):\n        self.brightness = brightness\n\ndesk = Lamp(4)\nprint(desk.brightness)',
               "4",
               ["class Lamp: introduces a new type; its indented block defines that type's behavior.", "Lamp(4) creates an instance and runs __init__ with the new instance and brightness 4.", "self.brightness stores 4 on that instance. desk is the caller's name for the instance."],
               "Which is the class, which is the instance, and which is the attribute?"),
        lesson("self means the particular object receiving the call",
               "self is a conventional parameter name, not a separate global object. Python supplies it when a method is called through an instance. desk.adjust(2) passes desk as self and 2 as amount. That is why method definitions include self even though callers do not type it.\n\nA parameter like amount is temporary input for one call. An attribute like self.brightness stays on the instance after that call returns. Two instances share method code but normally keep their own instance attributes. Put mutable collections inside __init__ when each instance needs its own collection; a list created directly in the class body is shared.",
               'class Lamp:\n    def __init__(self, brightness):\n        self.brightness = brightness\n\n    def adjust(self, amount):\n        self.brightness += amount\n\ndesk = Lamp(4)\nhall = Lamp(9)\ndesk.adjust(2)\nprint(desk.brightness, hall.brightness)',
               "6 9",
               check="In desk.adjust(2), what does self refer to? Why does hall remain at 9?"),
        lesson("Initialization and methods have different jobs",
               "__init__ has two underscores on each side; it is a special name Python recognizes. It establishes starting state. Ordinary methods perform later operations. A method can return a result just like any function; changing an attribute and returning a value are separate decisions. A method with no return gives back None even if it changed state.\n\nClasses can be defined at module level. They can also be defined inside a function: the indented class definition creates the local type when that function runs. One exercise asks for that local arrangement. Follow its requested wrapper and return value so the browser can call it; do not replace the required function with only a top-level class.",
               check="Explain the difference between constructing Lamp(4), reading desk.brightness, and calling desk.adjust(2)."),
        lesson("Guard an invariant before changing state",
               "An invariant is a promise that stays true after every accepted operation. If a container cannot hold more than its capacity, calculate the proposed state first, reject invalid changes, then store an accepted result. Rejection should leave the previous state intact.\n\nState the input assumptions too. A check against going above capacity says nothing about negative additions unless the contract handles them. Tests must include rejected operations and confirm the state afterward. A successful return alone cannot prove a rejection left the object unchanged.",
               'class WaterJug:\n    def __init__(self, capacity):\n        self.capacity = capacity\n        self.level = 0\n\n    def pour(self, amount):\n        proposed = self.level + amount\n        if amount < 0 or proposed > self.capacity:\n            return False\n        self.level = proposed\n        return True\n\njug = WaterJug(6)\nprint(jug.pour(4), jug.level)\nprint(jug.pour(3), jug.level)',
               "True 4\nFalse 4",
               ["The first proposal fits and becomes the new state.", "The second proposal would be 7, beyond capacity 6.", "The early return happens before assignment, so level remains 4."],
               "What assertion would detect a rejected operation that accidentally changed the level?"),
        lesson("Separate normalization, selection, and ordering",
               "A pipeline is a sequence of small steps where each output becomes the next input. In a text search, decide which values should be normalized for comparison and which original values should be returned. For example, stripping whitespace and lowercasing a search term does not require replacing every original display string.\n\nSubstring membership uses small_text in larger_text. It is different from item membership in a list. sorted(strings) returns a new alphabetically ordered list and leaves the source list alone. An empty substring is contained in every string, so the specification must decide whether an empty query is allowed. Functions with one clear responsibility are easier to combine and test than one long function doing all stages.",
               'labels = ["Studio", "Kitchen", "study"]\nneedle = "  STU ".strip().lower()\nselected = []\nfor label in labels:\n    if needle in label.lower():\n        selected.append(label)\nprint(sorted(selected))\nprint(labels)',
               "['Studio', 'study']\n['Studio', 'Kitchen', 'study']",
               check="Why do the results retain their original capitalization even though the comparison ignores case?"),
        lesson("Best-per-key data: missing is not always zero",
               "A dictionary can store one best-so-far value for each distinct key. Before updating, distinguish 'this key has never appeared' from 'its current best is zero'. A default of zero would incorrectly beat legitimate negative measurements. Membership with key in mapping answers existence; lookup answers the stored value.\n\nDecide whether you want a total, latest value, or maximum. Those are different update policies. In your lab, document the policy and test an inferior update after a superior one, a new key, and tied values. Keep storage separate from ranking so a change in presentation does not alter the recorded state.",
               'coldest = {"north": -4}\nprint("south" in coldest)\nprint("north" in coldest, coldest["north"])\nprint(coldest.get("south", 0))\n# The returned 0 is a fallback, not an actual south measurement.',
               "False\nTrue -4\n0",
               check="Why would treating a missing key as a real measurement of zero give a wrong best-so-far result?"),
        lesson("A ranking key is a tuple of priorities",
               "Review the chapter-3 sorting rule: sorted receives a key function, and calls it for each item. A tuple compares its first field, then its second only when the first ties. Negating a numeric field changes its direction within an otherwise ascending key. A name used as a second field makes ties deterministic.\n\nDefine a named key function first so each operation is visible. The equivalent lambda is a short anonymous function: lambda item: expression. It has a parameter, a colon, and one returned expression. It is optional shorthand, not a different sorting algorithm. Do not negate text. Decide tie policies from the task rather than copying a key from another example.",
               'boxes = [{"label": "B", "weight": 4}, {"label": "A", "weight": 4}, {"label": "C", "weight": 7}]\ndef box_priority(box):\n    return (-box["weight"], box["label"])\nordered = sorted(boxes, key=box_priority)\nfor box in ordered:\n    print(box["label"], box["weight"])',
               "C 7\nA 4\nB 4",
               check="Write the three key tuples by hand. Why does A appear before B?"),
        lesson("Inheritance is a later option, not a requirement",
               "The compact material mentions inheritance. class Child(Parent): defines a specialized type that can reuse the parent's methods. Overriding means defining a method with the same name in the child. super() gives access to the parent's implementation for the current instance.\n\nUnderstand the call sequence before using it. This example changes the volume step of a speaker; it does not require reimplementing initialization. Inheritance is useful for a genuine 'is a kind of' relationship. An ordinary helper function or an object stored in an attribute is often simpler. You do not need inheritance for the main chapter exercises.",
               'class Speaker:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.volume = 0\n\n    def turn_up(self):\n        self.volume += 2\n\nclass QuietSpeaker(Speaker):\n    def turn_up(self):\n        super().turn_up()\n        self.volume -= 1\n\nradio = QuietSpeaker()\nradio.turn_up()\nprint(radio.volume)',
               "1",
               ["QuietSpeaker inherits Speaker.__init__, so construction sets volume to zero.", "The child method calls the parent method first: volume becomes 2.", "Its own adjustment then makes volume 1."],
               "Which method runs when radio.turn_up() is called, and why?"),
        lesson("Special methods connect objects to Python syntax",
               "Names such as __str__, __len__, and __eq__ are special methods. Python calls __str__ to obtain a readable string, __len__ for len(object), and __eq__ for equality. Return the kind of value the operation requires: text for __str__, a nonnegative integer for __len__, and an equality result for __eq__.\n\nWithout a custom equality policy, ordinary user-created objects usually compare by identity. If you implement equality, check the other object's type before reading its attributes. isinstance(value, Type) asks whether value is an instance of that type or a subtype. NotImplemented is a special response meaning this comparison method does not support the other type. These extension hooks are optional here; first make your state and method calls clear.",
               'class Marker:\n    def __init__(self, color):\n        self.color = color\n\n    def __str__(self):\n        return f"Marker: {self.color}"\n\n    def __eq__(self, other):\n        if not isinstance(other, Marker):\n            return NotImplemented\n        return self.color == other.color\n\nprint(Marker("blue"))\nprint(Marker("blue") == Marker("blue"))',
               "Marker: blue\nTrue"),
        lesson("Lab readiness: choose responsibilities before code",
               "For your independent score service, write down who owns stored state, which operation updates it, which operation ranks it, and which layer prints or saves it. A class is optional unless the exercise explicitly requires one. A plain data transformation can remain a function.\n\nIf you use classes, create two instances in a test and change only one to prove their state is separate. Test ties, negative values where allowed, and rejected updates. When refactoring a long function, first capture its behavior in tests; change the structure while preserving its inputs, outputs, and documented side effects. A neat class diagram cannot replace those observable promises.",
               check="Can a caller know whether an operation mutates state from your function name and documentation? If not, state that policy explicitly."),
    ],
    11: [
        lesson("A tool starts with an input/output promise",
               "A complete tool connects several smaller operations. Before implementation, choose one ordinary input and its exact expected output. Name the accepted types, missing-value policy, duplicate policy, ordering, and failure behavior. 'Supports import' is a feature name; 'reads these records and produces this table' is a testable promise.\n\nWork in stages: read external input, validate and normalize it, combine values, order results, format output. Keep the middle stages as ordinary functions returning ordinary Python values. File access and terminal printing belong at the outer boundary. You can then test decisions without having to create files or launch an interactive program for every case.",
               check="Write a tiny example involving two records that refer to the same thing with different capitalization. What duplicate policy will your tool promise?"),
        lesson("Normalize once, without losing the display value",
               "Normalization chooses a consistent representation for comparisons: stripping outer whitespace, making text lowercase, and converting numeric text deliberately. It should follow the specification, not silently invent a new identity rule. Two labels can normalize to the same key, so preserve a separate display label if the user needs original capitalization.\n\nA chain such as text.strip().lower() first calls strip, then calls lower on the returned string. Both return new strings. int(text) converts numeric text and raises ValueError for invalid numeric text; only skip error handling when the contract guarantees conversion. Missing keys, None, and empty strings are different inputs and may need different policies.",
               'raw = {"label": "  Morning Walk ", "steps": "120"}\ndisplay = raw["label"].strip()\nkey = display.lower()\nsteps = int(raw["steps"])\nprint(display)\nprint(key, steps)\nprint(raw["label"] == "  Morning Walk ")',
               "Morning Walk\nmorning walk 120\nTrue",
               check="Which values are useful for display, and which for identity matching? Did the original record change?"),
        lesson("Unique cleaned values: loop first, comprehension second",
               "To normalize a collection, first write the explicit loop: clean one item, reject an empty result, and add acceptable results to a set. set.add(value) keeps one instance of each unique value. Sets do not promise presentation order, so sort them when returning a list.\n\nA set comprehension uses {expression for item in collection if condition}. Read it as: visit each item, keep those passing the condition, compute the expression, and collect unique results. It is a compact form of a loop, not required syntax for the task. A dictionary comprehension instead has a colon between a key and a value. Use the loop if repeating normalization in a short expression makes it less clear.",
               'labels = [" Blue ", "", "BLUE", "Green"]\nunique = set()\nfor label in labels:\n    clean = label.strip().lower()\n    if clean:\n        unique.add(clean)\nprint(sorted(unique))\ncompact = {label.strip().lower() for label in labels if label.strip()}\nprint(sorted(compact))',
               "['blue', 'green']\n['blue', 'green']",
               ["The first blue is inserted; the second blue is already present and creates no duplicate.", "The empty string is false, so it is not added.", "Both implementations return the same sorted presentation; the loop makes each stage explicit."],
               "What distinguishes {} from set()? Why would returning a set directly make output order unreliable?"),
        lesson("Grouping combines records according to a policy",
               "A group key says which records belong together. A dictionary can hold one accumulated value per key. The update policy might sum quantities, preserve the latest value, or keep the maximum; these policies are not interchangeable. Decide it before writing the update.\n\nmapping.get(key, default) returns a value without inserting the default. An assignment is still needed to store an updated value. Trace the first occurrence separately from later occurrences: the first starts from the documented default, while later ones use the previous accumulated value. Do not mutate the original records unless the contract explicitly permits it.",
               'deliveries = [("north", 2), ("south", 4), ("north", 3)]\nboxes_by_stop = {}\nfor stop, boxes in deliveries:\n    before = boxes_by_stop.get(stop, 0)\n    boxes_by_stop[stop] = before + boxes\n    print(stop, boxes_by_stop[stop])',
               "north 2\nsouth 4\nnorth 5",
               check="If this policy were 'largest delivery' rather than 'all boxes delivered', which conceptual step would change?"),
        lesson("CSV writing: let the library handle commas and quotes",
               "CSV is text containing rows and fields. A field can itself contain a comma, quotation mark, or newline. Joining values with commas cannot represent all those cases correctly. csv.writer(file_like_object) creates a writer that quotes fields when necessary. writer.writerow(sequence) writes one row.\n\nio.StringIO() is an in-memory text stream: it behaves like a small text file without touching disk. It starts empty. The writer adds text to it, and getvalue() retrieves the complete string. repr(text) displays escape sequences such as \\r\\n so you can inspect exact line endings. The output below shows the representation of the string; the returned CSV itself contains real line breaks.",
               'import csv\nimport io\noutput = io.StringIO()\nwriter = csv.writer(output)\nwriter.writerow(["place", "visits"])\nwriter.writerow(["Park, east", 2])\ntext = output.getvalue()\nprint(repr(text))',
               "'place,visits\\r\\n\"Park, east\",2\\r\\n'",
               ["The first row supplies two headers.", "The comma inside Park, east belongs to one field, so the writer surrounds that field with quotes.", "The default writer line terminator is carriage return plus newline; tests may deliberately check those exact characters."],
               "Why would ','.join(...) misrepresent the place in this example?"),
        lesson("Dictionary comprehensions and stable exports",
               "A dictionary comprehension produces key/value entries: {key_expression: value_expression for item in collection}. The colon separates each key from its value. Dictionary keys must be unique; if two generated entries have the same key, the later value replaces the earlier one. That makes a comprehension unsuitable for aggregation unless you have already resolved duplicates.\n\nIteration over a dictionary yields its keys. sorted(mapping) therefore gives sorted keys, not values or key/value pairs. To export a deterministic report, choose an ordering explicitly and look up each key's value. This preserves the mapping and separates aggregation from presentation.",
               'length_by_word = {word: len(word) for word in ["pear", "fig"]}\nprint(length_by_word)\nfor word in sorted(length_by_word):\n    print(word, length_by_word[word])',
               "{'pear': 4, 'fig': 3}\nfig 3\npear 4",
               check="Why would a dictionary comprehension alone lose information if repeated records must have their quantities summed?"),
        lesson("A safe entry point: import without starting the program",
               "Python executes a module's top-level statements when it is first imported. A test importing your functions should not immediately ask for input or overwrite an export. Put the run sequence in main(), then use a main guard.\n\n__name__ is a module variable set by Python. It equals the string '__main__' when that file is launched directly; it is the module's import name when imported. The comparison if __name__ == '__main__': therefore runs main only in the direct-launch case. Defining functions and classes at import time is normal; performing unrelated external actions at import time makes reuse and testing difficult.",
               'def headline(text):\n    return text.upper()\n\ndef main():\n    print(headline("weekly notes"))\n\nif __name__ == "__main__":\n    main()',
               "WEEKLY NOTES",
               check="Which lines define reusable behavior and which line starts the run? What should happen when a test imports headline?"),
        lesson("Tests describe behavior, not the current implementation",
               "A useful assertion compares the observed result with an answer independently chosen from the specification. unittest discovers methods whose names start with test_ inside unittest.TestCase subclasses. self.assertEqual(actual, expected) reports a failure when the values differ. The self here is the test instance; it is unrelated to the object your program may be testing.\n\nUse one small input that exposes a specific bug. A duplicate test should actually contain duplicates after normalization. A quoted-field test needs text requiring quoting. An empty test should check the chosen output shape, not just that nothing crashes. The example runs a self-contained test case directly; in your lab keep tests in test_core.py and run unittest discovery from that folder.",
               'import unittest\n\ndef first_letter(text):\n    return text.strip()[0:1]\n\nclass LetterTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_outer_spaces(self):\n        self.assertEqual(first_letter("  seed "), "s")\n\ncase = LetterTests("test_outer_spaces")\nresult = unittest.TestResult()\ncase.run(result)\nprint(result.testsRun, result.wasSuccessful())',
               "1 True",
               check="What wrong implementation would this specific test catch? What empty-input test would you add?"),
        lesson("From functions to a local CLI, one command at a time",
               "A CLI is a command-line interface: the terminal supplies arguments, the program performs a task, and output goes back to the terminal. A minimal first version can choose an action from a small list of argument strings before you study argparse in chapter 13. Keep the command choice outside your normalization and export functions.\n\nsys.argv contains strings: index 0 is the launched script name, and sys.argv[1:] is the remaining argument list. 'list' and 'export' are strings your program chooses to interpret. Use small synthetic files in a separate lab folder. For CSV files opened locally, pass newline='' and encoding='utf-8'; the CSV module should control line endings. For JSON, use the parse/write techniques from chapter 6. Start with one working command, then add the others rather than writing every branch at once.",
               'import sys\n# A real CLI reads sys.argv[1:]. This fixed list demonstrates its shape.\narguments = ["list", "sample.json"]\ncommand = arguments[0]\npath_text = arguments[1]\nprint(command, path_text)',
               "list sample.json",
               check="What should happen if no command was supplied? Write that policy before indexing an argument list."),
        lesson("Lab handoff: another person should reproduce your result",
               "The independent lab asks you to build import, list, search, and CSV export using your own design. Before writing it, choose exact input formats, normalization and duplicate rules, and ordering. Reuse your tested functions rather than putting every calculation in the CLI. Include a tiny synthetic dataset with ordinary, duplicate, empty, and quoted-text examples.\n\nYour README should contain setup commands, actual run commands, representative output, test commands, limitations, and help used. Test instructions from a clean folder or environment, since hidden dependencies on your current directory can make a correct function unusable as a tool. The supplied checkpoint tests cover one slice of the project; passing them does not prove the full import/export workflow.",
               check="Can you explain which layer owns file access, which owns normalization, and which owns printing?"),
    ],
    12: [
        lesson("Independence means choosing steps, not guessing syntax",
               "This chapter combines earlier ideas into new contracts. Reduced guidance should mean you decide the plan yourself; it should not mean a new language feature is hidden in an example. Review the needed building blocks here, then close the examples before solving the tasks. Looking up syntax is normal.\n\nStart with one input and exact output, then write the steps in ordinary words. Separate validation, grouping, filtering, ranking, and presentation. For each step, name the data shape it receives and produces. A dictionary of totals is a different shape from a list of [name, total] pairs. Many bugs happen when one stage returns the wrong shape for the next.",
               check="For your chosen project, write down the input shape and output shape without writing Python."),
        lesson("Unpack pairs and include categories with zero activity",
               "A record such as ['east', 'west'] is a two-item list. Writing first, second = record assigns its two items to separate names; it requires exactly two items. A loop can unpack each record in its header.\n\nWhen several roles appear in a record, think about which roles must exist in your result. Some entities may need a zero entry even when they never receive an increment. 'Not in the mapping' and 'present with zero' convey different facts. Establish the population separately from deciding which values change. Then the accumulation policy can remain simple.",
               'routes = [["pier", "park"], ["park", "hill"]]\nplaces = set()\nfor origin, destination in routes:\n    places.add(origin)\n    places.add(destination)\nprint(sorted(places))',
               "['hill', 'park', 'pier']",
               check="Which place would be lost if you recorded only destinations? Which if you recorded only origins?"),
        lesson("Split once when the remainder may contain separators",
               "str.split(separator, maxsplit) limits how many cuts are made. split('|', 1) cuts only at the first vertical bar, preserving any later bars inside the message. Without the limit, you may get more fields than expected. Before unpacking into two names, decide what to do when no separator exists.\n\n.strip() removes outer whitespace, while .upper() returns uppercase text. Applying them to a category label does not imply you should change the message too. An allow-list is a collection of permitted values checked with membership. It is useful when unknown labels must be ignored or reported rather than silently accepted.",
               'line = "  notice | open gate | slowly"\nparts = line.split("|", 1)\nlabel = parts[0].strip().upper()\nmessage = parts[1].strip()\nprint(label)\nprint(message)\nprint(label in {"NOTICE", "ALERT"})',
               "NOTICE\nopen gate | slowly\nTrue",
               check="What is returned by 'no separator'.split('|', 1)? Why would blindly reading parts[1] fail?"),
        lesson("Counting first, then scanning in original order",
               "Some tasks need a global fact before deciding which original item qualifies. A counting pass can build how often each value occurs. A later scan can then inspect that count while preserving original input order. These are two passes, not a nested scan of the entire input for every item.\n\nTwo O(n) passes still take O(n) time: doubling the input roughly doubles the total work. The count mapping needs storage for distinct values. Do not confuse dictionary iteration order with an arbitrary ranking policy; when the answer is defined by original order, scan the original sequence explicitly. Decide how to represent 'nothing qualifies' before writing a return.",
               'letters = "moss"\nfrequency = {}\nfor letter in letters:\n    frequency[letter] = frequency.get(letter, 0) + 1\nprint(frequency)\nfor letter in letters:\n    print(letter, frequency[letter])',
               "{'m': 1, 'o': 1, 's': 2}\nm 1\no 1\ns 2\ns 2",
               check="How many dictionary lookups happen in the second pass? How would that compare with recounting the whole string for every character?"),
        lesson("Rank by several fields, then apply the limit",
               "A multi-field tuple key expresses priority: compare the first component, use the second to break ties, then the third if needed. A negative numeric component sorts larger numeric values first while leaving later text components ascending. Use a named function when that makes the rule easier to explain.\n\nFiltering, ranking, and limiting are separate operations and usually need that order. Taking the first few inputs before sorting can discard a better candidate. A slice [:limit] selects up to that many items from the sorted result. If negative limits are not meaningful, validate or clamp according to your contract rather than accidentally using Python's negative-slice meaning.",
               'rooms = [{"name": "Birch", "space": 12, "walk": 3}, {"name": "Ash", "space": 12, "walk": 1}, {"name": "Cedar", "space": 8, "walk": 1}]\ndef room_key(room):\n    return (-room["space"], room["walk"], room["name"])\nordered = sorted(rooms, key=room_key)\nfor room in ordered[:2]:\n    print(room["name"])',
               "Ash\nBirch",
               check="Write the key tuples by hand. Which comparison is decided by space, and which by walking distance?"),
        lesson("Read compact syntax only after the steps are clear",
               "A lambda is a one-expression function: lambda item: expression means receive item and return expression. Passing it with key= supplies a sorting rule. A list comprehension [expression for item in items] builds a new list from each result. These are abbreviations for a function and a loop you can already write explicitly.\n\nIf a compact line combines filtering, lookup, sorting, and formatting, expand it before debugging. Put intermediate results in well-named variables and print their values or inspect them in a debugger. Shorter source code does not imply a simpler explanation or faster algorithm.",
               'weights = {"small": 2, "large": 7}\nordered = sorted(weights, key=lambda name: (-weights[name], name))\npairs = [[name, weights[name]] for name in ordered]\nprint(pairs)',
               "[['large', 7], ['small', 2]]",
               ["Iterating weights yields the names small and large.", "The key function looks up each weight and reverses only that numeric priority.", "The comprehension builds a new two-item list for each already-ordered name."],
               "Can you replace the lambda with a named function and the comprehension with a loop without changing the result?"),
        lesson("Test transfer with a changed requirement",
               "A remembered answer is useful recall, but transfer means adapting the reasoning when a rule changes. After completing a task, choose one small variation: a different tie-break rule, a different permitted category, a different limit, or a different duplicate policy. Write a new expected result before changing code.\n\nInclude empty inputs, exact boundaries, duplicates, and ties where relevant. Preserve input when the contract says so: make a separate snapshot before calling your function, then compare afterward. Test assumptions too. For example, an input contract allowing negative values needs tests that would expose a default of zero. Record any help honestly and revisit the work later from a blank file.",
               check="Name one test that distinguishes your intended ranking rule from a plausible but wrong rule."),
        lesson("Independent lab: a small specification and evidence",
               "Choose one of the chapter's project directions, then define a narrow useful version. Your written specification should state valid input, expected output, duplicate and tie behavior, invalid-input policy, and what is deliberately outside scope. A useful limitation is specific, such as supporting one documented file format rather than all spreadsheets.\n\nKeep the implementation, tests, examples, loop trace or algorithm explanation, and run instructions together. State one tradeoff: for example, a clearer two-pass implementation may use more memory than a streaming approach. Do not label yourself ready or unready based on one exercise. Evidence that you can explain, debug, test, and modify the result is the point of this chapter.",
               check="Could another person reproduce your example without asking which working directory or input file you used?"),
    ],
    13: [
        lesson("The command boundary: terminal, interpreter, script, arguments",
               "A terminal accepts commands. Its current directory decides how relative paths are resolved. cd changes that directory; dir on Windows or ls on macOS/Linux lists its contents. The interpreter is the Python executable that reads and runs your script. Everything after the script name is an argument supplied to that script.\n\nIn python report.py --copies 3, python is the interpreter, report.py is the file, and --copies plus 3 are argument strings. Do not type these commands into a .py file or at Python's >>> prompt. Save code in the editor, then run the command from the terminal in the folder containing the file. Paths containing spaces need quotes when supplied as a single command argument.",
               check="In your terminal, identify the current directory and the Python executable you intend to use before running anything."),
        lesson("An environment selects the interpreter and installed packages",
               "A virtual environment is a project-specific Python installation area. It lets this project use its own packages rather than accidentally relying on packages installed for another project. Creating .venv does not move your code into it. Keep source files beside .venv, not inside it. The VS Code interpreter selection and your terminal command should use the same environment.\n\nFrom the lab folder on Windows, create it with py -m venv .venv and run its interpreter as .\\.venv\\Scripts\\python.exe. On macOS/Linux, create it with python3 -m venv .venv and use ./.venv/bin/python. You do not need to activate it when you use that explicit path. -m means run an installed Python module as a command. Use the environment's interpreter with -m pip when installing a dependency; the standard library needs no installation.",
               check="What does the explicit interpreter path tell you that a bare pip command does not?"),
        lesson("argparse creates a parser object and declares options",
               "argparse is Python's built-in command-line parsing module. ArgumentParser() creates a parser object. parser.add_argument(...) declares an accepted option; it does not yet read user input. A keyword such as default=2 names an argument to add_argument. type=int passes the int conversion function itself, so the parser can convert incoming text.\n\nparse_args(list_of_strings) reads those strings and returns a Namespace object. Its attributes contain parsed values. In type(args.copies).__name__, type finds the value's type and __name__ reads that type's name as text, such as int. Passing a list makes examples and tests deterministic. In a real CLI, parse_args() with no explicit list reads the terminal arguments. The automatically provided --help explains declared options and exits. This chapter's browser task supplies valid arguments; a full CLI should also be tried with invalid input.",
               'import argparse\nparser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description="Choose print copies")\nparser.add_argument("--copies", type=int, default=2)\nargs = parser.parse_args(["--copies", "3"])\nprint(args.copies, type(args.copies).__name__)\nprint(parser.parse_args([]).copies)',
               "3 int\n2",
               ["The declaration says that --copies takes a value converted with int.", "The text '3' becomes integer 3 on the returned Namespace.", "An empty list supplies no override, so the default is used."],
               "Why is type=int written without parentheses? What would int('3') do immediately instead?"),
        lesson("Flags are present-or-absent choices",
               "A flag such as --quiet does not need a following value. action='store_true' sets its result to True when present and leaves it False when absent. Hyphens in long option names become underscores in the Namespace attribute by default: --show-path is read as args.show_path.\n\nDo not pass the text 'False' to a store_true flag: its presence already means true. A dry-run flag is a product promise, not magic provided by argparse. Your program must use its boolean value to print a proposed plan without applying changes. Keep plan calculation separate from the part that writes files so the flag can reliably suppress side effects.",
               'import argparse\nparser = argparse.ArgumentParser()\nparser.add_argument("--show-path", action="store_true")\nprint(parser.parse_args([]).show_path)\nprint(parser.parse_args(["--show-path"]).show_path)',
               "False\nTrue",
               check="Which part of your code must enforce 'preview only': argument parsing, planning, or applying changes?"),
        lesson("Git has a working tree, staging area, and history",
               "Your working tree contains files you can edit. The staging area holds the exact snapshots selected for the next commit. A commit records those staged snapshots in local history. git add file.py updates the staged snapshot at that moment. If you edit the file afterward, the new edit is not automatically part of that snapshot.\n\nUse git diff to compare working files with the staging area, and git diff --staged to compare the staging area with the previous commit. git status summarizes all three. A remote is another repository, often on GitHub. git push uploads commits; it does not create the local commit or include uncommitted edits. You already practiced the basic flow in earlier labs; this chapter explains why it works.",
               check="If you stage a file and then edit it again, which version would git commit record? Which diff would show the later edit?"),
        lesson("Read the two status columns separately",
               "git status --short represents each tracked file with two status columns. The first describes the staging area compared with the last commit; the second describes the working tree compared with staging. A space means no change in that column. MM can mean a staged modification plus a later unstaged modification in the same file. Count these independently.\n\nThe special prefix ?? means an untracked file; it is not two ordinary modification columns. A leading space matters, so do not strip the line before examining status positions. Your exercise receives simplified status strings with a fixed two-character prefix. It is a parser exercise, not an attempt to execute Git inside the browser.",
               'examples = ["M  notes.py", " M notes.py", "MM notes.py", "?? draft.py"]\nfor line in examples:\n    print(repr(line[:2]), line[3:])',
               "'M ' notes.py\n' M' notes.py\n'MM' notes.py\n'??' draft.py",
               check="Which example represents both staged and unstaged work? Why would line.strip() destroy useful information?"),
        lesson("Branches, commits, and pull requests describe different steps",
               "A branch is a named line of commits. Creating one lets you make a focused change without moving your main branch immediately. git switch -c name creates and switches to a branch; git switch name switches to an existing branch. A pull request is a review proposal on GitHub, comparing branch changes. Opening a pull request does not itself merge the changes.\n\nFor the local lab, make a small tested change in your own learning repository. Review the diff, commit only intended files, push the branch, and open a practice pull request in GitHub. Give it a description of the problem, resulting behavior, and checks. If a conflict appears, stop and inspect both intended changes rather than deleting unfamiliar markers or force-pushing. The first-time workspace guide remains the source for repository setup and sign-in.",
               check="Explain which actions are local and which require communicating with GitHub."),
        lesson("Configuration arrives as text, including booleans",
               "An environment variable is a named setting provided to a process by its surroundings. os.environ is a mapping of those names to strings. .get(name, default) uses the default only when the key is missing; an invalid existing value should not silently become the default unless that is the documented policy.\n\nThe nonempty strings '0' and 'false' are both truthy, so bool(text) does not parse a boolean setting. Compare against allowed text values instead. Convert integer settings explicitly, catch the expected conversion error, then validate the allowed range. A test can pass an ordinary dictionary to your parsing function; this avoids changing the machine's real environment and makes tests reproducible.",
               'settings = {"RETRIES": "0", "VERBOSE": "false"}\nraw = settings.get("RETRIES", "2")\nretries = int(raw)\nverbose = settings.get("VERBOSE", "").lower() in ("1", "true")\nprint(retries, verbose)\nprint(bool(settings["VERBOSE"]))',
               "0 False\nTrue",
               check="How should your function distinguish a missing setting, text that cannot convert, and a converted number outside the allowed range?"),
        lesson("Exit codes are a second output channel",
               "A command prints messages for people and returns an exit code for other programs. Zero normally means success; nonzero means some failure category chosen by the tool. Your core function can return a result dictionary describing the code and message; a real command boundary translates that result into printing and process exit.\n\nraise SystemExit(code) asks Python to end the process with that code. Put it under the main guard so importing the module does not exit the test process. In this example, main returns a code; merely calling main does not automatically exit. The browser exercises model exit decisions as data so they can be tested without closing the worker.",
               'def main():\n    print("preview complete")\n    return 0\n\ncode = main()\nprint("returned code:", code)\n# A local entry point can use raise SystemExit(main()).',
               "preview complete\nreturned code: 0",
               check="Why should a conflict take priority over a preview-success message when both conditions are present?"),
        lesson("Secrets and generated files belong outside version history",
               "A .gitignore lists untracked paths Git should ignore, such as .venv/, __pycache__/, local output, and secret configuration files. It does not remove a file already tracked or erase a secret from earlier commits. If a real secret was committed, revoke or rotate it; deleting the current line is insufficient. Do not put real keys in a practice dataset or terminal transcript.\n\nBefore your lab commit, inspect git status, git diff, and git diff --staged. Test --help, defaults, explicit arguments, and the preview mode. Record the supported Python version and exact environment commands in the README so another person can reproduce your setup without packages installed elsewhere.",
               check="Can your dry run be tested without changing real files? Can your configuration be tested without editing the actual environment?"),
    ],
    14: [
        lesson("A test has arrange, act, and assert stages",
               "Arrange creates a small controlled input. Act performs the operation. Assert compares what happened with the promised result. Keep the expected result independent of the implementation: copying its calculation into the test can reproduce the same bug. A useful test should distinguish correct behavior from at least one plausible wrong behavior.\n\nPython's assert condition raises AssertionError when the condition is false. An optional message after a comma explains the failure. Assertions are useful in tests; they are not appropriate runtime validation for untrusted input because Python can disable them with optimization. Validation should use explicit conditions and documented returns or exceptions.",
               'def twice_length(text):\n    return len(text) * 2\n\n# Arrange\ntext = "oak"\nexpected = 6\n# Act\nactual = twice_length(text)\n# Assert\nassert actual == expected, "wrong doubled length"\nprint("passed")',
               "passed",
               check="What plausible bug does this test catch? Would it detect a problem that occurs only on an empty string?"),
        lesson("The test runner discovers methods, not individual assertions",
               "unittest discovers TestCase methods whose names start with test_. One method may contain several assertions, so 'Ran 2 tests' means two methods ran, not necessarily two example inputs. Separate methods give clearer failure names. subTest labels individual cases inside a loop without treating each case as a discovered method.\n\nassertEqual compares values, assertTrue checks a condition, and assertRaises checks an expected exception. Exception testing matters because a function can promise to reject bad input. Do not catch the exception inside the test unless you are checking it deliberately; otherwise an unintended exception can be hidden as a passing test.",
               'import unittest\n\ndef letters(text):\n    if not isinstance(text, str):\n        raise TypeError("text required")\n    return len(text)\n\nclass LengthTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_bad_type(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(TypeError):\n            letters(None)\n\ncase = LengthTests("test_bad_type")\nresult = unittest.TestResult()\ncase.run(result)\nprint(result.testsRun, result.wasSuccessful())',
               "1 True",
               check="Why would a test that accepts any exception be weaker than one expecting TypeError?"),
        lesson("pytest uses ordinary test functions",
               "pytest is a third-party test runner, not a built-in Python keyword or part of unittest. Files named test_*.py contain functions named test_*. Each function can use plain assert statements. Keep imports separate from running a program: the tested module's main guard should prevent input prompts or file writes during import.\n\nIn your local .venv on Windows, install it with .\\.venv\\Scripts\\python.exe -m pip install pytest, then run .\\.venv\\Scripts\\python.exe -m pytest -q. On macOS/Linux use ./.venv/bin/python in those commands. The existing lab's unittest tests continue to work; pytest can discover them too. Record the dependency for a clean environment. Browser teaching does not install pytest or contact a package server.",
               check="What makes a function discoverable by pytest? Why should you invoke pip and pytest through the same environment's interpreter?"),
        lesson("pytest.raises and parametrize introduce a context and a decorator",
               "pytest.raises(ExpectedError) is a context manager. Place the operation expected to fail inside its with block. The test fails if the operation does not raise the expected error. This is the same testing idea as unittest.assertRaises, expressed through pytest.\n\nA decorator is an @ line immediately above a function that supplies information or changes how that function is used. @pytest.mark.parametrize lists example arguments so pytest runs the test once per supplied tuple. The first string names the test parameters; each tuple supplies their values in that order. These are local test-file illustrations, not commands to paste into the terminal.",
               '# Local file test_lengths.py; requires pytest installed in .venv.\nimport pytest\n\n@pytest.mark.parametrize("text, expected", [("oak", 3), ("", 0)])\ndef test_lengths(text, expected):\n    assert len(text) == expected\n\ndef test_bad_integer():\n    with pytest.raises(ValueError):\n        int("not a number")',
               shell=True,
               check="How many parameterized examples are supplied? Which operation is required to raise ValueError?"),
        lesson("An error object has a type and a message",
               "raise stops normal execution and passes an exception to the caller. A caller can catch a specific exception with except ErrorType as error; the name error then refers to that exception object. str(error) obtains its message as text. Keep the type and text distinct: the type lets a caller choose which failure to handle, while the message explains the specific case.\n\nA custom exception class can name a domain-specific problem. class MissingSeat(Exception): pass creates such a type by inheriting the standard Exception behavior. pass is an empty statement used where a block is required. Catch the narrowest expected error and let unrelated programming mistakes surface.",
               'class MissingSeat(Exception):\n    pass\n\ndef seat_label(seats, number):\n    if number not in seats:\n        raise MissingSeat(f"seat {number} unavailable")\n    return seats[number]\n\ntry:\n    seat_label({1: "window"}, 2)\nexcept MissingSeat as error:\n    print(type(error).__name__)\n    print(str(error))',
               "MissingSeat\nseat 2 unavailable",
               check="Why could except Exception hide a misspelled variable or a different bug you did not intend to handle?"),
        lesson("A fake dependency is an ordinary callable you control",
               "A dependency is something your function relies on: a clock, database, or network fetcher. Dependency injection means passing that dependency in rather than hiding it inside the function. Because functions are values, you can pass a function as an argument, then call the received parameter.\n\nA fake is a simple substitute with predictable behavior. It should follow the same input/output interface as the real dependency. A list of calls can record interactions as well as returned values. This lets you prove that a cached value prevented a fetch, for example, rather than merely proving that the final answer happened to be right. Prefer this small explicit fake to complicated mocking of internal implementation details.",
               'def show_time(clock):\n    return f"time: {clock()}"\n\ncalls = []\ndef fake_clock():\n    calls.append("clock")\n    return "09:30"\n\nprint(show_time(fake_clock))\nprint(calls)',
               "time: 09:30\n['clock']",
               ["fake_clock is passed without parentheses so show_time receives the function itself.", "clock() inside show_time calls the supplied function.", "The fake returns controlled text and records that it was called once."],
               "What would change if you passed fake_clock() instead of fake_clock?"),
        lesson("Existence, truthiness, and emptiness are different questions",
               "A dictionary can contain a key whose value is 0, False, None, or an empty collection. key in mapping tests existence. mapping.get(key) retrieves a value, but testing that value's truthiness does not answer whether the key exists. Cache code often breaks when it confuses these questions.\n\nSimilarly, a task asking for a value other than None and the empty string is not asking for a truthy value. [] and 0 are false in conditions but may be allowed answers. is None checks the special absence object by identity; == '' checks an empty string. Use the exact exclusion policy in the contract rather than a broader if value shortcut.",
               'stored = {"attempts": 0, "answer": None}\nprint("attempts" in stored, bool(stored["attempts"]))\nprint("answer" in stored, stored["answer"] is None)\nprint("missing" in stored, stored.get("missing") is None)',
               "True False\nTrue True\nFalse True",
               check="Why do the second and third lookups both produce None even though only one key exists?"),
        lesson("Type hints and docstrings document an interface",
               "A type hint is a note to readers and analysis tools. In def label(text: str) -> str, : str documents the parameter and -> str documents the return. These annotations do not automatically reject incorrect runtime input. You still validate untrusted values when your contract requires it.\n\nA docstring is the first string inside a function or class. Triple quotes let it span lines. Explain what the function accepts, returns, changes, and may raise. A useful docstring describes behavior rather than narrating every assignment. Type hints express shapes; the docstring explains policies such as whether empty input is allowed or the original collection is preserved. The function attribute __doc__ holds its docstring as text; printing frame.__doc__ shows the documentation, not the function's returned value.",
               'def frame(text: str) -> str:\n    """Return text inside brackets; do not change its content."""\n    return f"[{text}]"\n\nprint(frame("note"))\nprint(frame.__doc__)',
               "[note]\nReturn text inside brackets; do not change its content.",
               check="Would annotating an argument as int automatically prevent a caller from passing a string?"),
        lesson("Debug by checking one hypothesis at a time",
               "Start from a reproducible discrepancy: input, expected result, actual result. Reduce to the smallest case that still fails. State a hypothesis about the line responsible, then inspect values at that line. A temporary print is enough for a small loop. A debugger breakpoint pauses a local program so you can inspect variables before continuing.\n\nIn VS Code, set a breakpoint in the left margin and choose Run and Debug for the current Python file with the lab interpreter. 'Step over' runs a line, while 'step into' enters a called function. breakpoint() also opens Python's local debugger, but do not put it in browser exercises or unattended tests. After finding the issue, remove temporary debugging code and keep a regression test that failed before the correction.",
               check="For your lab's falsy-value bug, which variable and condition would you inspect at the breakpoint?"),
        lesson("Lab: prove the failure and the correction separately",
               "Your lab asks you to introduce or reproduce a small parser or cache bug, write a failing test, inspect the wrong decision, and make a focused correction. First watch the test fail for the intended reason. A syntax error or bad test import is not proof that it caught the logic bug. Then run the corrected test and the rest of the suite.\n\nUse a fake dependency to check both the stored-value case and the absent-key case. Record call counts or arguments where relevant. Add public-function type hints and docstrings, then document the reproduction command, diagnosis, changed behavior, test results, and limitations. No test suite proves every possible behavior; explain which risk each example addresses.",
               check="Can you explain what would fail if the old buggy implementation were restored?"),
    ],
    15: [
        lesson("Parsed data is not necessarily valid data",
               "JSON parsing proves that text follows JSON syntax. It does not prove that a record has the fields, types, or ranges your application needs. Validate in an order that makes each later operation safe: verify a required key or choose a missing policy, check its type, normalize text, then check the allowed value.\n\nCalling .strip() on None raises an error before your intended validation can respond. Comparing an absent quantity with zero has the same problem. Use small independent validators so you can test each rejection rule. Keep the original source and a row position when reporting errors, so someone can repair the actual input rather than guessing which record failed.",
               'row = {"label": None}\nvalue = row.get("label")\nif isinstance(value, str):\n    print(value.strip())\nelse:\n    print("label must be text")',
               "label must be text",
               check="Why must the type check happen before normalization? What result does .get produce for a missing key?"),
        lesson("Python booleans are also a special kind of integer",
               "isinstance(value, int) accepts ordinary integers and booleans because bool is a subclass of int. That relationship is useful in some Python arithmetic but can be surprising in record validation: a quantity of True often should not mean one item.\n\nWhen the policy requires exactly an integer and excludes booleans, type(value) is int checks the exact type. is compares object identity; int here is Python's integer type object. Do not use exact-type checks everywhere automatically: choose them only when the input contract requires this distinction. A nonnegative range check must still happen after the type check.",
               'print(isinstance(True, int))\nprint(type(True) is int)\nprint(type(3) is int)\nprint(True + 1)',
               "True\nFalse\nTrue\n2",
               check="Which two checks would a policy need to accept zero but reject both negative integers and booleans?"),
        lesson("A regular expression describes a small text grammar",
               "A regular expression, or regex, describes allowed patterns in text. Python's re module supplies the functions; the pattern itself is a string. Start with literal characters: BIN- in a pattern means those exact characters. [A-Z] means one ASCII uppercase letter, [0-9] one ASCII digit, and {2} means exactly two repetitions of the preceding part. The r before the quote creates a raw string: it keeps backslashes for the regex engine rather than having Python consume them first. This example has no backslash, but the convention prepares for patterns that do.\n\nre.fullmatch(pattern, text) requires the entire input to match. It returns a match object on success and None on failure; bool(...) turns those into True and False. A prefix-only match could accept unwanted suffixes, so use fullmatch when validating a whole identifier. Do not strip or lowercase the input unless the format explicitly allows those changes.",
               'import re\npattern = r"BIN-[0-9]{2}"\nprint(bool(re.fullmatch(pattern, "BIN-47")))\nprint(bool(re.fullmatch(pattern, "BIN-470")))\nprint(bool(re.fullmatch(pattern, "bin-47")))',
               "True\nFalse\nFalse",
               ["The literal prefix requires uppercase BIN followed by a hyphen.", "[0-9]{2} requires two ASCII digits, not an arbitrary length.", "fullmatch rejects the extra digit and the wrong-case prefix."],
               "Which input would distinguish fullmatch from checking only whether the start matches?"),
        lesson("Raw strings, captures, and replacement have separate meanings",
               "A raw string has r before its opening quote. It keeps most backslashes as literal characters for the regex engine, instead of Python consuming them first. Regex \\d matches Unicode decimal digits, which is broader than ASCII [0-9]. Regex \\w includes Unicode word characters and underscore; it is not simply English letters. + means one or more repetitions.\n\nParentheses make a capture group. re.findall returns all matched strings, or captured pieces when the pattern has a capture. re.sub(pattern, replacement, text) returns a new string with matching regions replaced. Learn each operation separately; a pattern that validates one field need not parse a complete document, HTML, or a calendar date.",
               'import re\nprint(re.findall(r"@([A-Za-z]+)", "ask @Mia or @Bo"))\nprint(re.sub(r"[0-9]+", "#", "batch 12, shelf 4"))\nprint(bool(re.fullmatch(r"[0-9]+", "１２")))',
               "['Mia', 'Bo']\nbatch #, shelf #\nFalse",
               check="In the first pattern, why are the returned names missing the @ sign? What does + permit?"),
        lesson("Calendar dates need a calendar parser",
               "A string cannot safely calculate month lengths or leap days. datetime.date represents a calendar date. date.fromisoformat(text) parses an ISO date; date(year, month, day) constructs one from integer components. Invalid calendar values raise ValueError. The chapter exercises guarantee their date inputs, but your local validator should document and handle rejected dates.\n\nSubtracting two date objects returns a timedelta: a duration. Its .days attribute is a signed integer, so reversing the subtraction changes the sign. Adding timedelta(days=...) moves through the calendar correctly. Date objects do not include a time of day or timezone; they represent calendar dates rather than worldwide instants.",
               'from datetime import date, timedelta\nstart = date.fromisoformat("2024-02-28")\nfinish = start + timedelta(days=2)\nprint(finish.isoformat())\nprint((finish - start).days)\nprint((start - finish).days)',
               "2024-03-01\n2\n-2",
               check="Why is subtracting parsed dates safer than subtracting their day-of-month text?"),
        lesson("Date presentation and timezone policies are explicit choices",
               "isoformat() turns a date object back into stable year-month-day text. strftime(format) uses formatting directives: %Y is a four-digit year, %m a two-digit month, and %d a two-digit day. Month-name directives can depend on the machine's locale, so numeric ISO output is easier to reproduce.\n\nA datetime includes a time of day. A timezone-aware datetime also identifies how that local time relates to UTC. A naive datetime lacks that timezone information. Do not compare or combine them without an explicit policy. The current date-only practice needs date, not a timestamp. If your project later records worldwide instants, use aware UTC values internally and convert for display rather than guessing a local timezone.",
               'from datetime import date, datetime, timezone\nday = date(2025, 1, 6)\nprint(day.strftime("%Y/%m/%d"))\ninstant = datetime(2025, 1, 6, 9, 30, tzinfo=timezone.utc)\nprint(instant.isoformat())',
               "2025/01/06\n2025-01-06T09:30:00+00:00",
               check="Does a birth date necessarily need a timezone? Does a timestamp for an online purchase need a timezone policy?"),
        lesson("Money needs a representation and a precision policy",
               "Binary floating-point numbers cannot represent every decimal fraction exactly. The small difference in 0.1 + 0.2 matters when an application promises exact currency arithmetic. Store whole cents as integers, or use decimal.Decimal constructed directly from text. Constructing Decimal from an already-rounded float preserves that float's approximation instead of recovering the original decimal amount.\n\nDecimal('1.20') represents a decimal quantity. Multiplying a validated amount by 100 converts currency units to cents. int(...) truncates rather than rounding, so that conversion is valid only after you have enforced a suitable precision policy. The browser task guarantees at most two fractional digits. For a local validator, decide whether extra digits are rejected or rounded, and document the chosen rounding rule.",
               'from decimal import Decimal\nprint(0.1 + 0.2)\namount = Decimal("1.20") + Decimal("0.35")\nprint(amount)\nprint(int(amount * 100))',
               "0.30000000000000004\n1.55\n155",
               check="Why is Decimal('1.20') safer than Decimal(1.20) when the user supplied decimal text?"),
        lesson("Sum Decimal values from a Decimal starting point",
               "sum(collection, start) begins with start, then adds every item. The second argument is optional and defaults to integer zero. Passing Decimal('0') makes the intended numeric type explicit and gives an empty collection a Decimal result too. A generator expression can supply converted values one at a time: (conversion(item) for item in items). This is the same mapping idea as a list comprehension without constructing a list first.\n\nYou can also use a plain loop with a Decimal accumulator if that is clearer. Do not convert each decimal to float as an intermediate step. Validate each field first; malformed decimal text can raise decimal.InvalidOperation, a specific exception from the decimal module.",
               'from decimal import Decimal\nweights = ["0.75", "1.25"]\ntotal = sum((Decimal(text) for text in weights), Decimal("0"))\nprint(total)\nprint(sum([], Decimal("0")))',
               "2.00\n0",
               check="For an empty input, what value and type should your public function return under its contract?"),
        lesson("Rejected records need positions and reasons",
               "enumerate(rows) supplies a zero-based position and each row. Keeping that position lets a rejection report point back to the source. A reason should name the violated rule, such as 'quantity must be an integer', without dumping unnecessary private data. Count every row in an explicit category so accepted plus rejected equals total input; if duplicates are a third category, include them in the reconciliation.\n\nA browser contract may request only rejected positions, while a local tool can include reasons too. Preserve the required browser return shape rather than adding extra fields that the tests did not request. In your lab, write independent identifier, date, and price parsers; test every rejection rule separately before connecting them into a record pipeline.",
               'rows = [{"label": "box"}, {"label": ""}]\nrejected = []\nfor index, row in enumerate(rows):\n    if not row["label"]:\n        rejected.append({"row": index, "reason": "empty label"})\nprint(rejected)',
               "[{'row': 1, 'reason': 'empty label'}]",
               check="If you show humans one-based spreadsheet row numbers, where will you document the conversion from zero-based indexes?"),
        lesson("Lab readiness: malformed, invalid, and out of scope",
               "Malformed text cannot be parsed; parsed-but-invalid data violates your application rules; out-of-scope data belongs to a format your tool does not promise to support. Keep those distinctions visible. Include a leap-day example, invalid calendar date, missing field, boolean quantity, extra identifier character, and a currency precision boundary in your tests when those rules apply.\n\nWrite accepted and rejected outputs without changing the original source. Record the date format, timezone assumptions, currency representation, and rounding or rejection policy in README. Your program should help a person repair input and should reconcile every input row. You do not need one enormous regex or one enormous try block to do that; small validators with specific contracts are easier to explain and test.",
               check="Can each rejected example be linked to one documented rule, with a focused test proving that rule?"),
    ],
    16: [
        lesson("Tables, rows, columns, and a schema",
               "A relational database stores structured tables. A row is one record; columns name its fields. A schema defines those tables and constraints. TEXT stores text, INTEGER stores whole numbers, and a primary key uniquely identifies a row. A foreign key identifies a related row in another table; it is not a copy of that row's display name.\n\nSQL is the language used to define, query, and change tables. A SQL statement is text interpreted by the database, not Python syntax. Python sends it through the sqlite3 library. Start by creating a tiny in-memory database: the special path ':memory:' means temporary data inside this connection, with nothing saved after it closes.",
               'import sqlite3\nconnection = sqlite3.connect(":memory:")\nconnection.execute("CREATE TABLE shelves (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, label TEXT)")\nprint("table ready")\nconnection.close()',
               "table ready",
               check="Which part of the example is Python and which part is SQL? Why does closing this database lose its data?"),
        lesson("A connection sends statements; a cursor holds results",
               "sqlite3.connect(...) returns a connection object. connection.execute(statement, parameters) sends one statement and returns a cursor. For a SELECT query, the cursor lets you retrieve result rows. fetchall() returns a list of row tuples. fetchone() returns one row tuple, or None if no row remains. A query selecting one column still returns one-item tuples such as ('east',), not bare strings.\n\nclose() releases the connection. Use explicit cleanup after the example's operations. In a real program, a try/finally can close it even when an operation raises an error. A connection's with block controls transaction commit or rollback; it does not automatically close that connection. Those are separate responsibilities.",
               'import sqlite3\nconnection = sqlite3.connect(":memory:")\nconnection.execute("CREATE TABLE shelves (label TEXT)")\nconnection.execute("INSERT INTO shelves VALUES (?)", ("east",))\ncursor = connection.execute("SELECT label FROM shelves")\nrows = cursor.fetchall()\nprint(rows)\nprint(rows[0][0])\nconnection.close()',
               "[('east',)]\neast",
               check="What are the types of rows, rows[0], and rows[0][0]?"),
        lesson("Bind values with placeholders, including the one-item tuple",
               "The ? in SQL is a parameter placeholder. Pass values separately as a tuple or sequence: execute('... WHERE label = ?', (wanted,)). The comma in (wanted,) makes it a one-item tuple. (wanted) alone is only parentheses around a value, not a tuple.\n\nParameter binding keeps a quote inside a value from turning into SQL syntax. Never use an f-string or string concatenation to insert external values into SQL. Parameters stand for values, not table or column names; choose identifiers from trusted program code or an explicit allow-list. Binding is one key protection, alongside permissions, schema rules, and input policies; it is not a complete security system by itself.",
               'import sqlite3\nconnection = sqlite3.connect(":memory:")\nconnection.execute("CREATE TABLE shelves (label TEXT)")\nwanted = "O\'Neil"\nconnection.execute("INSERT INTO shelves VALUES (?)", (wanted,))\nrow = connection.execute("SELECT label FROM shelves WHERE label = ?", (wanted,)).fetchone()\nprint(row)\nconnection.close()',
               '("O\'Neil",)',
               check="Why should a title containing SQL-looking text still be treated as one ordinary value?"),
        lesson("Insert several rows with executemany",
               "executemany(statement, rows) repeats one parameterized statement for each supplied row. Each inner tuple or list supplies one set of parameter values in column order. The number of values must match the number of placeholders. This is more reliable than building a SQL string by joining external data.\n\nName columns in INSERT statements when clarity matters; column order then becomes explicit. A Python list of pairs is not already a database table: inserting transfers those values into the table's rows. Validate or choose a rejection policy before inserting real-world records, and use constraints for important database invariants too.",
               'import sqlite3\nconnection = sqlite3.connect(":memory:")\nconnection.execute("CREATE TABLE parcels (label TEXT, mass INTEGER)")\nrows = [("blue", 6), ("amber", 3), ("green", 8)]\nconnection.executemany("INSERT INTO parcels (label, mass) VALUES (?, ?)", rows)\nprint(connection.execute("SELECT COUNT(*) FROM parcels").fetchone()[0])\nconnection.close()',
               "3",
               check="How many times is the INSERT statement applied, and which input shape supplies one execution?"),
        lesson("SELECT, WHERE, and ORDER BY answer different questions",
               "SELECT chooses returned columns. FROM chooses the source table. WHERE filters rows according to a condition. ORDER BY requests an output order; without it, do not rely on the sequence rows happen to arrive in. ASC is ascending and is the default; DESC is descending. Multiple ORDER BY fields specify tie-break priorities.\n\nFiltering in SQL does not change the table. The result is a new sequence of rows. If your Python contract requires a list of strings, explicitly extract each row's first item rather than returning the cursor or tuple rows unchanged. Keep the expected return shape visible at the boundary.",
               'import sqlite3\nconnection = sqlite3.connect(":memory:")\nconnection.execute("CREATE TABLE parcels (label TEXT, mass INTEGER)")\nconnection.executemany("INSERT INTO parcels VALUES (?, ?)", [("blue", 6), ("amber", 3), ("green", 8)])\nrows = connection.execute("SELECT label FROM parcels WHERE mass > ? ORDER BY label", (4,)).fetchall()\nlabels = []\nfor row in rows:\n    labels.append(row[0])\nprint(labels)\nconnection.close()',
               "['blue', 'green']",
               check="Which clause determines whether a row qualifies, and which determines its position in the result?"),
        lesson("GROUP BY produces one result per group",
               "An aggregate combines several rows: SUM adds values, COUNT counts rows or non-NULL values, and MAX chooses a greatest value. GROUP BY separates input rows into groups before calculating an aggregate for each group. WHERE filters individual rows before grouping. HAVING filters groups after the aggregate has been calculated.\n\nEvery selected non-aggregate column should identify the group clearly. ORDER BY still matters after grouping. With no matching rows, COUNT returns zero, while SUM can return SQL NULL. NULL means missing or unknown; it is not zero. Decide whether a zero replacement is meaningful before using one.",
               'import sqlite3\nconnection = sqlite3.connect(":memory:")\nconnection.execute("CREATE TABLE deliveries (district TEXT, boxes INTEGER)")\nconnection.executemany("INSERT INTO deliveries VALUES (?, ?)", [("east", 2), ("west", 1), ("east", 3)])\nquery = "SELECT district, SUM(boxes) FROM deliveries GROUP BY district HAVING SUM(boxes) >= ? ORDER BY district"\nprint(connection.execute(query, (2,)).fetchall())\nconnection.close()',
               "[('east', 5)]",
               check="Why does east contribute one result row even though it has two input rows?"),
        lesson("JOIN connects records through keys",
               "Suppose a table holds one row per location and another holds many visits. A JOIN combines matching rows using a relationship condition. SQL aliases are short temporary names: places AS p lets p.id mean the id column from places. ON describes which rows match, often a child foreign key equals a parent primary key.\n\nAn INNER JOIN includes only matching pairs. A LEFT JOIN keeps every row from the left table, even when the right side has no match. The missing right-hand columns are NULL. One left row can appear several times if several child rows match. This multiplicity is expected; account for it before counting or summing.\n\nThe setup below uses executescript to run several fixed SQL statements separated by semicolons. Triple quotes let the Python string span lines. Keep this setup script trusted and fixed; bind external values with execute or executemany, not by adding them to the script text.",
               'import sqlite3\nconnection = sqlite3.connect(":memory:")\nconnection.executescript("""CREATE TABLE places (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, label TEXT);\nCREATE TABLE visits (place_id INTEGER, people INTEGER);\nINSERT INTO places VALUES (1, \'garden\'), (2, \'lake\');\nINSERT INTO visits VALUES (1, 2), (1, 3);""")\nquery = "SELECT p.label, v.people FROM places AS p LEFT JOIN visits AS v ON v.place_id = p.id ORDER BY p.label, v.people"\nprint(connection.execute(query).fetchall())\nconnection.close()',
               "[('garden', 2), ('garden', 3), ('lake', None)]",
               ["Garden matches two visit rows, so it appears twice before aggregation.", "Lake has no visit row; LEFT JOIN preserves it with a missing right-hand value.", "sqlite3 translates SQL NULL to Python None."],
               "Which row would disappear under INNER JOIN? Why would COUNT(*) count the preserved lake row as one?"),
        lesson("COALESCE and COUNT handle missing children deliberately",
               "COALESCE(expression, fallback) uses the first value that is not SQL NULL. It is not a general falsy-value check: a numeric zero remains zero. COUNT(child_id) ignores NULL values, whereas COUNT(*) counts every joined row. For a left-side row with no child, this distinction separates zero real children from one preserved placeholder row.\n\nSQL uses IS NULL or IS NOT NULL to test missingness. A comparison such as value = NULL does not act like a Python value is None check. WHERE keeps rows whose condition is true, so use the correct NULL test.\n\nWhen a report promises to include entities with no activity, start from the full parent table and use a LEFT JOIN. Choose whether a missing aggregate should be zero or remain unknown. SQL NULL and Python None represent missingness at the boundary; the meaning must come from your application's policy.",
               'import sqlite3\nconnection = sqlite3.connect(":memory:")\nrow = connection.execute("SELECT COALESCE(NULL, 0), COALESCE(0, 99), COUNT(*) FROM (SELECT 1)").fetchone()\nprint(row)\nconnection.close()',
               "(0, 0, 1)",
               check="Why would checking a value's truthiness in Python fail to model COALESCE accurately?"),
        lesson("Foreign-key constraints protect relationships",
               "A foreign key says a child value must refer to an existing parent key. In the schema clause FOREIGN KEY(place_id) REFERENCES places(id), place_id is the child column and places.id is its allowed source. Both are SQL column names, not Python variables. Enabling enforcement matters: SQLite connections need PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON before related data is inserted.\n\nAn invalid relationship raises sqlite3.IntegrityError rather than quietly creating an orphan. This does not replace a useful validation message at the program boundary; it is a final consistency rule shared by every caller. The example demonstrates only the schema and one rejected relationship. Your lab must choose its own entities, fields, insertion functions, and error policy.",
               'import sqlite3\nconnection = sqlite3.connect(":memory:")\nconnection.execute("PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON")\nconnection.execute("CREATE TABLE places (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY)")\nconnection.execute("CREATE TABLE visits (place_id INTEGER, FOREIGN KEY(place_id) REFERENCES places(id))")\ntry:\n    connection.execute("INSERT INTO visits VALUES (?)", (9,))\nexcept sqlite3.IntegrityError:\n    print("missing parent")\n    connection.rollback()\nconnection.close()',
               "missing parent",
               check="Why is checking an existing parent's name in Python weaker than enforcing the relationship by its stable ID in the database?"),
        lesson("Transactions keep related changes together",
               "A transaction is a group of database changes treated as one unit. commit() accepts the changes. rollback() undoes uncommitted changes. If two related inserts must succeed together, commit only after both succeed; on an expected database failure, roll back. A connection's with block can perform commit or rollback on exit, but the connection must still be closed separately.\n\nConstraints such as UNIQUE and FOREIGN KEY protect data even when a caller makes a mistake. In SQLite, enable foreign-key enforcement on the connection with PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON before starting related operations. A constraint failure raises a specific sqlite3 exception. Catch failures you have a plan to report rather than hiding every programming error.",
               'import sqlite3\nconnection = sqlite3.connect(":memory:")\nconnection.execute("CREATE TABLE labels (name TEXT UNIQUE)")\nconnection.commit()\ntry:\n    connection.execute("INSERT INTO labels VALUES (?)", ("blue",))\n    connection.execute("INSERT INTO labels VALUES (?)", ("blue",))\n    connection.commit()\nexcept sqlite3.IntegrityError:\n    connection.rollback()\n    print("rolled back")\nprint(connection.execute("SELECT COUNT(*) FROM labels").fetchone()[0])\nconnection.close()',
               "rolled back\n0",
               ["The schema exists before the demonstrated transaction begins.", "The second insertion violates UNIQUE, so the first insertion must not remain half-applied.", "rollback removes the first pending insertion; the final count proves neither remained."],
               "Why would committing immediately after the first insertion prevent the intended all-or-nothing result?"),
        lesson("File persistence, indexes, and migrations are separate concerns",
               "A file path such as 'practice.sqlite3' creates or opens a persistent database rather than a ':memory:' database. It is relative to the process's current directory. Commit intended changes, close the connection, reopen the same path, and query again to prove persistence. Use only a disposable lab folder and synthetic data while learning.\n\nAn index is an additional lookup structure that can speed selected queries; it also consumes storage and costs work on writes. It does not replace correct WHERE or ORDER BY clauses. A migration changes an existing schema or stored representation and needs a plan for old data; recreating an empty table is not a migration. Your lab should test quoted input as data, zero-activity reports, transaction rollback, and close/reopen persistence. Keep schema, query, and return-shape decisions documented.",
               check="Which of your tests need a fresh in-memory database, and which specifically need a temporary file database? Why?"),
    ],
}
